    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Okay, thanks for that. So it sounds like if I accidentally leak something or fail to secure a file, I'm on the hook for everything from the actual damage to their lawyers' fees. Is there any way to limit this liability, or is it just a standard \"catch-all\" for any mistakes I might make?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While the indemnification clause does provide broad protection for the Disclosing Party, there are some limitations and nuances to consider.\n\nIn general, the clause is designed to cover \"reasonable\" attorneys' fees, which means that the Disclosing Party can only recover fees that are reasonable and proportionate to the losses they've incurred. This is often referred to as the \"reasonable attorney's fees\" limitation.\n\nAdditionally, the clause may also have specific exclusions or limitations, such as:\n\n* Exclusions for certain types of losses or damages, such as consequential damages or punitive damages\n* Limitations on the amount of damages that can be recovered\n* Requirements for notice or cooperation from the Recipient before the Disclosing Party can pursue indemnification\n\nIt's also worth noting that the clause may be subject to specific conditions or requirements, such as:\n\n* The Recipient must have acted in good faith and without gross negligence\n* The Recipient must have taken reasonable steps to prevent or mitigate the losses\n* The Disclosing Party must have given the Recipient reasonable notice of the losses or damages\n\nIn terms of limiting liability, you may be able to negotiate some of these limitations or exclusions as part of the agreement. However, it's generally best to consult",
